A||||2||theogre||HOW TO: Ignition module install.||||1||
Z||000000||theogre||04-07-2000||08:38 PM||||HOW TO: Ignition module install.<P>This applies to all GM distributor mounted and DIS Primary Ignition Modules(PIM). It’s just a couple quick note to make sure they are installed right. (This HOW TO: is a summary of other postings.)<P>Distributor mounted PIM:<P>CLEAN the mounting area on the distributor. Most of the mounting area should be bright aluminum.<P>Apply a short line of Heat Sink compound to the center of the PIM.<P>Screw the PIM to the distributor. The heat sink compound will spread itself filling the air gaps.<P>Apply Dielectric grease to the terminals and connect the wires.<P>DIS PIM:<P>Yes I covered this by itself. This is the short version.<P>If there is any sign of oil leak under the brick remove the brick and replace the O ring or the whole crank sensor.<P>If the crank sensor is not leaking DO NOT remove the brick from the car.<P>To replace the PIM remove the coils. This is a pain if the brick is on the car but it can be done. <P>During removal you can pull up on the coil as you unscrew the nuts. During install the top left coil bolt will stay put ok. The top & bottom right you can hold by hand. The bottom left is easiest if you make something to reach up under the brick and hold the bolt in the hole. (They are carriage bolts. You only need to hold them up. They will not turn when held in the hole.) <P>The brick base must be clean. Bright aluminum in the PIM mounting area is preferred.<P>Place a dime size gob of Heat Sink compound in the middle of the brick base plate. Place a small gob of Dielectric on the end of the Crank sensor before installing the PIM.<P>Bolt everything back together.<P>Heat Sink compound:<P>The most common form of this is a Silicone and Zinc oxide based paste. You can get it at Radio Shack and many other Electronic supply shops. This compound is much more effective then dielectric at transferring heat out of the PIM. (Thermalloy, a major maker of electronics heat sinks rates the compound 4 times better than dielectric, 20 times better than nothing.)<P>Dielectric grease is a silicone base paste used mainly for water proofing electronics. It will act as a thermal transfer agent but this is not what it’s made for.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||04-07-2000||09:58 PM||||Just a quick reminder, More heat sink compound does not mean more heat tranfer. Actually the more compound the less heat is transfered! <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-07-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000002||theogre||04-08-2000||05:16 PM||||more heat sink compound usually means more mess. It will ooz out from under untill the exces is gone from between the parts.<P>What you have to watch out for is that you recheck how tight the screws are later. If you use to much compound it's easy to think the screws are tighter than they are. Then the excess compound ozzes out when it gets warm and lets the screws slack.||||reg||1
